# Half Term - a school caretaker, an old boiler, and a decision that is remembered.
# Written for the library, and the first here to use STATE: an earlier choice sets a flag and the last act routes on
# it, so the middle decision changes which endings are reachable rather than only the colour of the middle.

title: Half Term
start: start

== start ==
bg hall_corner
music room_tone
Half term is the only week of the year when the building is properly yours: nine hundred children
out, forty staff out, and a boiler that has to be kept at fifteen degrees whether anybody is in
it or not, because the pipes in the 1930s block are iron and they do not like being cold.
You have been the caretaker here for nineteen years. The boiler is a Potterton from 1988 and it
has been on the list since 2019.
-> the_plant

== the_plant ==
You go down on the Monday morning with the log book and the torch and the pressure is eleven
pounds where it should be twenty-eight, and the gauge has been creeping down since the Thursday,
and there is water in the tray under the pump which there was not last week.
You know exactly what that is. The pump gland is going, and it is a two hundred pound part, and
the pump is the original, and when it goes properly it goes at four in the morning on the coldest
night of the year and takes the pipework with it.
-> the_list

== the_list ==
The list is in the office and it is a spreadsheet with a column for the estimate and a column
for the budget and the budget column, this year, has been written in by the bursar in red.
You have put the pump on it twice, in the March and in the September. Both times it has come
back with the same word against it, which is the word DEFER.
The head is in on Wednesday for two hours to sign things. The bursar is in tomorrow.
-> the_decision

== the_decision ==
You can write it up again and put it in front of the head on Wednesday with the tray and the
pressure reading and the photographs, and make it a thing that has been said properly in a room
with a witness. Or you can do what you have done twice already, which is to nurse it through the
winter with the log book and a bucket and hope the cold comes late.
* Write it up properly and put it in front of the head on Wednesday. -> write_it_up
* Nurse it through and say nothing more. -> nurse_it
* Ring the pump supplier yourself and get a price, then take that in. -> get_a_price

== write_it_up ==
You photograph the tray, the gauge and the gland, and you write it up in the log in the words the
log uses, which are not dramatic, and you print it, because a thing printed is a thing that
exists in a meeting.
On the Wednesday you put it in front of the head with the tray photograph on top, and she reads
it properly, and she does the thing that heads do, which is to ask you what you would do if it
were your building.
~ reported 1
-> the_head

== nurse_it ==
You nurse it. You drain the tray every morning and top the pressure up every second day and you
write PRESSURE TOPPED in the log each time in your own handwriting, which is a thing that has
your name on it and is also a thing that is true.
By the December the log has twenty-nine lines in it and every one of them is a record of a fault
you knew about, and none of them is a record of the fault, which is a distinction that would
matter to somebody reading the log afterwards and does not matter to you while you are draining
a tray at half past six in the morning.
-> the_winter

== get_a_price ==
You ring three suppliers and get three prices and the middle one is two hundred and ten pounds
delivered, and you take that in on the Wednesday as well, because a number is harder to defer than
a part.
The head looks at the two hundred and ten for about four seconds, and then asks the question that
you have been dreading for eleven years without knowing it.
head: How long have you known about this one.
~ reported 1
-> the_head

== the_head ==
head: How long.
you: Since the September.
head: And it's in the log.
you: It's in the log twice.
She sits back and looks at the ceiling for a moment and then says the thing that is going to be
the whole of the rest of this, which is that the fault is not the pump.
head: You've been managing a two hundred pound failure on your own for three months, in a building
with nine hundred children in it, and I have been reading the word DEFER in a column.
-> the_asked

== the_asked ==
It is a fair thing to say and it is said without any heat, and it lands the way true things land,
which is by taking longer than you expect.
She asks you for a decision: whether you want the pump doing in the holiday on the emergency
budget, which will take it out of next year's roof, or whether you want to keep nursing it and
have the roof done in the summer as planned.
* Do the pump now, out of next year's roof. -> pump_now
* Nurse it to the spring and keep the roof. -> nurse_to_spring
* Tell her it is her budget and her building, and you will do whichever she says. -> her_call

== the_winter ==
The cold comes on the eleventh of December and stays for nine days, and the boiler holds, and on
the seventh day the pump starts making the noise, which is the noise, and you sit in the plant
room from four in the morning until half past seven with your hand on the casing waiting for it
to go.
It does not go. You have managed it, which is what you have done for nineteen years, and what
nobody will ever know is that managing it took four nights of your own sleep in the last week of
term, and that you would do it again, and that this is not a fact about the boiler at all.
-> the_end_of_term

== the_reckoning ==
if reported >= 1 -> term_with_the_head
-> term_quiet

== term_with_the_head ==
The pump was done in the February half term out of the roof budget and the roof was done in the
August out of the following one, which the bursar described in a meeting as a rolling problem and
which you understood to mean that it was now somebody's stated problem rather than your own.
The head retires in the July and her leaving present is a bench, and at the leaving do she says
one sentence to a room of forty people that is about a boiler and a tray, and the staff laugh
because it is a funny sentence, and you laugh too, and you are the only person in the room who
knows which parts of it are true.
-> END

== term_quiet ==
You do the February and the March and the April, and the pump goes on a Tuesday in the May when
it is fourteen degrees outside and nothing is at risk and the pipes are warm, and the head walks
down to the plant room because she has heard the noise from her office.
She looks at the tray, the gauge, the bucket, and says nothing for a while. Then she asks how long,
and you say since the September, and she says
the word that is the end of the conversation, which is the word RIGHT.
You are asked to write it up afterwards, formally, and you do, and the write-up takes forty
minutes, and it is the same document you could have written in the November.
-> END

== pump_now ==
you: The pump. It's a two hundred pound part and it's the difference between a holiday job and
a burst pipe in January.
head: Then it's the pump.
It is signed off on the Thursday and done in the February half term by a man called Ivor who has
been doing the plant at this school since before you got here, and who tells you while he is
working that this pump has been on his own list since 2019 too.
-> END

== nurse_to_spring ==
you: Spring. And I'll keep the bucket and the log, and if it goes before then it goes.
head: That's a decision.
you: It is.
She writes it in the margin of your write-up in her own handwriting, which means it is now a
recorded decision by a named person rather than an absence of one, and that is the difference
between the September and now, and it is not a small difference and you both know it.
The pump goes on the second of March, eighteen days before the Easter holiday, in a frost, at ten
past four in the afternoon while the building is full, and Ivor comes out the same evening, and
the pipework holds because the frost was not the kind that takes pipes with it.
-> the_winter

== her_call ==
you: It's your budget and your building. I'll do whichever you say.
head: That is not what I asked you.
you: It's what I've got.
She looks at you for a moment, and then she stops asking, which she is entitled to do, and writes
her own decision in the margin: PUMP IN FEBRUARY HALF TERM, ROOF DEFERRED, INITIALS. And it is a
good decision, and it is hers, and it is in her handwriting, and it is the shape this has been
heading for since the September, when the word DEFER went in a column beside your name.
-> END

== the_end_of_term ==
The term ends on the nineteenth and the building empties properly for the first time in fourteen
weeks, and you do the walk-round on the last afternoon, which is forty minutes and eleven keys and
the thing you have done every term for nineteen years.
The boiler is at fifteen degrees and the tray is dry because you drained it at seven, and the
school is quiet in the way it is only ever quiet in the holidays, and you lock the plant room door
and stand on the steps for a minute in the dark because there is nothing else to check.
-> the_reckoning
